WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.036 --> 00:00:17.550
I was not surprised when Ned came to my cabin first thing the next morning.

2
00:00:17.551 --> 00:00:21.447
He looked very disappointed after our failed attempt at escape.

3
00:00:21.448 --> 00:00:26.955
"Well, Ned," I said, trying to comfort him. "Luck was against us last night."

4
00:00:26.956 --> 00:00:33.027
"I couldn't believe that the captain decided to stop the submarine at the very hour we intended to leave it."

5
00:00:33.028 --> 00:00:36.614
"Yes, he had to do some business at his bank."

6
00:00:36.615 --> 00:00:40.185
"His bank?" Ned gave me a curious look.

7
00:00:40.186 --> 00:00:45.352
"Captain Nemo keeps his wealth under the ocean, where it's safer than in any bank vault."

8
00:00:45.353 --> 00:00:49.852
I then told Ned everything that the captain had told me the previous evening.

9
00:00:49.853 --> 00:00:53.677
I also described all that I'd seen through the windows.

10
00:00:53.678 --> 00:01:01.011
I hoped my description of the captain's charity would persuade Ned not to abandon the submarine, but that was not the case.

11
00:01:01.012 --> 00:01:07.067
"It's not over yet," he said. "Another time we'll succeed, and tonight if necessary—"

12
00:01:07.068 --> 00:01:10.567
"What direction is the Nautilus heading?" I interrupted.

13
00:01:10.568 --> 00:01:13.003
"I don't know," said Ned.

14
00:01:13.004 --> 00:01:20.582
"We'll know for sure at noon when the lieutenant records our position on the map. Until then let's have no more talk of escape."

15
00:01:20.583 --> 00:01:24.797
The harpooner returned to his cabin, and I went into the gallery.

16
00:01:24.798 --> 00:01:28.551
The compass was not reassuring if we intended to escape.

17
00:01:28.552 --> 00:01:34.480
The Nautilus seemed to be going southwest, which meant that we were turning our backs on Europe.

18
00:01:34.481 --> 00:01:40.559
At about 11:30, the reservoirs were emptied, and the submarine rose to the surface.

19
00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.075
I rushed to the platform and found Ned already there.

20
00:01:44.076 --> 00:01:47.972
There was no land in sight, nothing but an immense sea.

21
00:01:47.973 --> 00:01:51.297
The air was foggy and the winds were whipping up.

22
00:01:51.298 --> 00:01:57.694
Ned kept trying to see through the thick fog, hoping that he'd spot some land, but there was nothing to see.

23
00:01:57.695 --> 00:02:04.297
At last he put down his telescope and ranted against Captain Nemo until the lieutenant came up to the platform.

24
00:02:04.298 --> 00:02:10.940
An hour later we were sailing underwater again. I returned to the gallery and consulted our position on the map.

25
00:02:10.941 --> 00:02:15.345
We were now more than three hundred miles from the nearest shore.

26
00:02:15.346 --> 00:02:21.091
There was no way to escape, and Ned was enraged when I informed him of our situation.

27
00:02:21.092 --> 00:02:27.099
As for me, I wasn't especially sorry that we'd had to abandon our dangerous plot for now.

28
00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:33.019
Relieved of my anxiety, I was able to return to my work with some degree of calmness.

29
00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:37.321
Late that night I received an unexpected visit from Captain Nemo.

30
00:02:37.322 --> 00:02:42.003
"I propose an unusual excursion, Professor," he said.

31
00:02:42.004 --> 00:02:47.916
"So far you've visited the ocean's depths only by daylight. Would you like to see them at night?"

32
00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:50.352
"Most certainly," I replied.

33
00:02:50.353 --> 00:02:53.590
"I must warn you that it will be a tiring experience.

34
00:02:53.591 --> 00:02:59.574
We'll have to walk a good distance and climb a mountain. The underwater 'roads' are rather rough."

35
00:02:59.575 --> 00:03:04.495
"You've only increased my curiosity with what you've said. I'm ready to follow you, Captain."

36
00:03:04.496 --> 00:03:08.400
He led the way to the room where we usually put on our diving suits.

37
00:03:08.401 --> 00:03:15.146
I was surprised to see that none of the crew was coming with us, and Captain Nemo had not invited Ned or Conseil.

38
00:03:15.147 --> 00:03:20.551
As we put on our suits, I wondered aloud where our electric lights were.

39
00:03:20.552 --> 00:03:28.479
"We won't need them," said Captain Nemo, "but you will need one of those." He pointed to a metal-tipped walking stick.

40
00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.614
It was almost midnight when we set foot on the bottom of the Atlantic Ocean.

41
00:03:32.615 --> 00:03:39.551
The waters were very dark, but the captain pointed to a red light shining brightly about two miles away.

42
00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:43.384
After a while the surface under our feet became slippery.

43
00:03:43.385 --> 00:03:47.130
I realized I was walking on seaweed-covered stones.

44
00:03:47.131 --> 00:03:53.607
My feet often slipped on this strange carpet, and I would have fallen more than once without my walking stick.

45
00:03:53.608 --> 00:03:58.702
As the rosy light that guided us grew brighter, I remained puzzled by it.

46
00:03:58.703 --> 00:04:04.863
Was I headed for some natural phenomenon that was unknown to man? Or was it made by man?

47
00:04:04.864 --> 00:04:10.273
Were we going to meet some friends of Captain Nemo who had also rejected living on land?

48
00:04:10.274 --> 00:04:16.686
I would not have been surprised to encounter one of those clusters of underwater houses that Captain Nemo had dreamed of.

49
00:04:16.687 --> 00:04:20.837
It was one o'clock in the morning when we arrived at the bottom of the mountain.

50
00:04:20.838 --> 00:04:25.003
But first we had to struggle through a grove of dead trees.

51
00:04:25.004 --> 00:04:31.336
Then we began our climb of the mountain. We climbed over rocks and jumped over crevices.

52
00:04:31.337 --> 00:04:35.227
Two hours after leaving the Nautilus, we were still climbing.

53
00:04:35.228 --> 00:04:39.592
We came upon some massive rocks that sheltered deep caves.

54
00:04:39.593 --> 00:04:48.751
My blood curdled whenever I saw an enormous antenna blocking my path or a sharp claw snapping shut in the shadow of some cavity.

55
00:04:48.752 --> 00:04:52.639
Millions of small spots shone brightly in the dark.

56
00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:57.894
These were the eyes of giant lobsters and crabs crouched in their holes.

57
00:04:57.895 --> 00:05:05.632
Captain Nemo, more used to these creatures than I was, seemed unbothered, pushing on until we reached a plateau.

58
00:05:05.633 --> 00:05:12.878
Below us lay some ruins, which to my surprise, looked as if they were made by man, not nature.

59
00:05:12.879 --> 00:05:19.957
There were vast heaps of stone among which I could definitely see the outlines of castles and temples.

60
00:05:19.958 --> 00:05:28.711
What was this? Where were we? I wanted to ask Captain Nemo, so I touched his arm, but he only pointed toward the mountain's peak.

61
00:05:28.712 --> 00:05:35.791
In a few minutes we reached the top, and looking down, I realized that this mountain was an underwater volcano.

62
00:05:35.792 --> 00:05:42.680
The glowing red light was really lava, and there below us was a town that the volcano had destroyed.

63
00:05:42.681 --> 00:05:47.196
Its roofs were open to the sky, and its temples had fallen.

64
00:05:47.197 --> 00:05:53.013
Its columns were lying on the ground. It was like an underwater version of Pompeii.

65
00:05:53.014 --> 00:05:59.442
I had to know where we were. I tried to speak, but Captain Nemo stopped me with a gesture.

66
00:05:59.443 --> 00:06:06.339
Picking up a chalk-like stone, he wrote one word on a black rock: Atlantis.

67
00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:14.077
The legendary lost continent from ancient times! I tried to fix in my mind every detail of this grand landscape.

68
00:06:14.078 --> 00:06:18.569
Captain Nemo remained motionless, leaning on a mossy stone.

69
00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:22.418
Was he dreaming of a civilization long since disappeared?

70
00:06:22.419 --> 00:06:29.396
Was it here that he—a man who wanted no part of modern life—came to steep himself in history?

71
00:06:29.397 --> 00:06:32.474
I wished I had access to his thoughts!

72
00:06:32.475 --> 00:06:38.561
At that moment the moon appeared above us and cast its pale rays on the buried continent.

73
00:06:38.562 --> 00:06:42.894
It was but a glimmer, but what an unbelievable sight!

74
00:06:42.895 --> 00:06:49.878
The captain stood up straight, took one last look over the ruins below us, and then signaled me to follow him.

75
00:06:49.879 --> 00:06:52.051
We quickly descended the mountain.

76
00:06:52.052 --> 00:07:00.124
Once we passed through the grove of dead trees, I could see the electric light of the Nautilus shining like a star to guide us home.

77
00:07:00.125 --> 00:07:04.378
Captain Nemo walked straight toward the ship, and we were soon onboard.

78
00:07:04.379 --> 00:07:09.353
I went to sleep thinking of all the amazing things I'd seen.
